INT. OBSERVATORY ROOF DOME - NIGHT

A hemisphere of dark wood and brass, the great shutter slid open on a spill of stars. In the center, a telescope the size of a cannon points at the sky. The floor is a compass rose of inlaid ebony. It is cold. Everyone's breath is visible.

CASSIAN LOWE, 40s, wrapped in a moth-eaten scarf, adjusts the telescope eyepiece with long inky fingers. He is an astronomer who has been alone with his work for fifteen years, and it shows in the way he does not quite look at people.

LYDIA ABERNATHY-OKAFOR, 30s, in a velvet coat, hair swept up and pinned with a single silver comb, stands at the foot of the iron stair, staring up at him. She has the stillness of someone who rehearsed a speech and forgot it on the climb.

And between them, curled on a worn leather chair, is MABEL, 70s, Cassian's mother, a tiny woman in knitted mittens, dozing, a cup of cold tea on her knee.

LYDIA
(to Cassian)
You didn't answer my letters.

CASSIAN
(not turning)
Comets keep their own hours.

LYDIA
I'm not a comet.

CASSIAN
No. A comet comes back.

Lydia flinches. She climbs one step.

LYDIA
(to the dome)
Seven years, and this is what it comes to. A man with his eye pressed to a hole.

MABEL
(half asleep)
That's a fine hole, dear.

CASSIAN
Mother.

MABEL
Just saying.

Lydia takes another step. Cassian's hand tightens on the brass. A star slides through the glass.

LYDIA
(to Cassian)
I'm marrying Edgar in June.

CASSIAN
Congratulations.

LYDIA
That's all?

CASSIAN
What would you like me to say?

LYDIA
Something. Anything that isn't polite.

CASSIAN
(muttering)
Edgar is a fool.

LYDIA
There it is.

CASSIAN
He wears spats to the beach.

LYDIA
He is kind.

CASSIAN
He is kind, and wears spats, and has never once looked up.

Lydia reaches the platform. She faces him. He finally turns. Their faces are a foot apart.

LYDIA
Cassian, look at me.

CASSIAN
I am looking.

LYDIA
Look at me, not past me.

He does. The eyes are wet. Behind them, a meteor streaks across the open shutter. Neither notices.

MABEL
(waking, to the room)
Did I miss something?

LYDIA
(not moving)
Everything, Mabel.

MABEL
(to the room)
I knew it. I always do.
